(window.SITE_PARTS=window.SITE_PARTS||{})["bill-college"]={"plain":{"bills":{"college":{"slug":"college","title":"The College Price Before Signing Day Act","sections":[{"anchor":"sec-0-1","heading":"\u00a7 0-1. Short title.","part":null,"body":"This Act may be cited as the \"College Price Before Signing Day Act.\""},{"anchor":"sec-0-2","heading":"\u00a7 0-2. Findings.","part":null,"body":"Congress finds as follows:\n\n1. This Act creates no new program of student assistance, appropriates no funds, and sets no tuition, fee, or other charge. It requires that a price be stated before a student is asked to commit to it, that a credit already paid for be applied to the requirement it satisfies, that the figures an institution publishes about itself match the figures it reports to the Federal Government, and that federal lending be sized to what a program's graduates earn rather than withdrawn from the students who come after them.\n\n2. Section 496(c)(9) of the Higher Education Act of 1965 requires an institution to have a transfer-of-credit policy, to disclose it publicly, and to state its criteria. No provision of federal law says what the policy must provide. An institution complies in full by publishing a policy under which no credit is ever applied to any requirement. Students who transfer lose an average of 43 percent of the credits they have earned and paid for, and 22 percent on the most common path, from a public two-year institution to a public four-year institution, counting only the credits the receiving institution did not accept. The loss does not end at acceptance: an institution may accept a course, enter it on the transcript as elective credit, count it toward the total credits required for a degree, and still require the student to take and pay for the same course again.\n\n3. An applicant admitted in December under a binding early program is obligated to enroll before any institution has stated what the year will cost. The admissions advantage purchased by that obligation is worth roughly 100 SAT points. Among applicants at the same tested band, 29 percent of those from families above $250,000 apply early, against 16 percent of those below $50,000, and students at private secondary schools apply early at three and a half times the rate of students at public ones. No institution can withdraw alone: Harvard University ended early action in 2006 and Princeton University dropped binding early decision immediately afterward, each stating that it hoped others would follow; none did, and both restored early programs in February 2011. The cost of unilateral withdrawal falls on the withdrawing institution's own applicants.\n\n4. Section 84001 of Public Law 119-21 conditions a program's eligibility for federal student loans, and then for all assistance under title IV, on the median earnings of its completers. The consequence falls on the students who enroll after the measurement, and an institution's cheapest compliance is to stop admitting students who might earn less. State performance-based funding is the tested analog, run across roughly thirty States for two decades; the systematic synthesis of the strongest causal studies found null-to-modest effects on retention and graduation, together with restricted access, gaming, and harm concentrated on underserved students and under-resourced institutions. A rule that keys a program's eligibility to a rate the regulated party partly controls will be met by controlling the rate. A limit on the amount that may be lent for a program is not that rule: it governs what may be borrowed rather than whether a student may enroll, it moves continuously rather than at a threshold, and a program brings itself back within it by charging less.\n\n5. Median annual debt service on the Department of Education's own program-level data is 4.9 percent of median earnings at the baccalaureate level, 7.1 percent at the master's level, and 17.0 percent at the first-professional level. A single earnings threshold cannot see that gradient. A limit on what may be borrowed for a program can, and it places the consequence on the amount rather than on the next student.\n\n6. Section 484B of the Higher Education Act of 1965 returns unearned student assistance in proportion to the part of the period completed, and then stops measuring at 60 percent. A student who withdraws on the last day of the sixtieth percent of a term has earned a proportion of the assistance; a student who withdraws the following morning has earned all of it. Nothing about what the student received has changed. The discontinuity also states the day on which an institution's financial interest in the student's continued enrollment ends.\n\n7. Institutions cannot leave a ranking. Reed College stopped cooperating with one national ranking in 1995 and was ranked anyway from assembled data, with the lowest available value assigned on indicators the publisher lacked; it fell from the top ten to the bottom quartile in a single year. Columbia University withdrew from an undergraduate ranking in 2022 and was unranked. In 2022 Columbia acknowledged misreporting figures to a ranking: average class size, the share of faculty holding terminal degrees, the student-faculty ratio, the share of faculty who are full time, and spending on instruction. Three of those \u2014 the student-faculty ratio, the full-time and part-time instructional staff counts from which the full-time share is computed, and spending on instruction \u2014 are separately reported to the Secretary of Education under section 487(a)(17) of the Higher Education Act of 1965; the Department's surveys do not collect class size or faculty degrees. The discrepancy is possible only because the two channels are permitted to disagree.\n\n8. The measured taste for consumption amenities among applicants is broad-based, while the taste for academic quality is confined to high-achieving students, and demand pressure of that kind accounts for about 11 percent of the variation across four-year institutions in the ratio of amenity spending to academic spending. An applicant on a campus visit can see the residence hall, the dining hall, and the stadium. No document the applicant receives states what share of the price pays for instruction. That is a shrouded attribute rather than a preference, and disclosure is the instrument it calls for.\n\n9. Section 4968 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 imposes an excise on the net investment income of certain private college endowments at rates of 1.4, 4, and 8 percent. It asks nothing of the institution and offers nothing for anything: an institution that halves what its students pay owes what an institution that doubles it owes. The figure that would let the charge be pointed at the price does not exist, because net price under section 132 of the Higher Education Act of 1965 is an average over first-time, full-time undergraduate students receiving aid, a subset whose size and composition the institution influences. A transfer student, a part-time student, a returning student, and a student who files no aid application are all outside it.\n\n10. Public Law 119-21 terminated the Grad PLUS program for new borrowers and capped graduate, professional, and parent borrowing. The marginal dollar of tuition is now borrowed privately. Section 523(a)(8)(B) of title 11, United States Code, added in 2005, excepts a private education loan from discharge on the same terms as a loan the public made or guaranteed. A loan that survives the borrower's bankruptcy almost without exception need not be priced on whether the borrower can repay it, and therefore need not be priced on what the program pays. Every other consumer lender in the United States underwrites because it bears the loss.\n\n11. Nothing in this Act sets, limits, or reviews any tuition, fee, or other charge; requires any institution to admit any applicant; requires any institution to change what it teaches; or conditions any institution's or program's eligibility to participate in a program under title IV of the Higher Education Act of 1965 on the earnings of its students. No provision of this Act is conditioned on an appropriation. The consumer testing of the aid offer, the obtaining and furnishing of earnings figures, and the publication of a program's median earnings depend on acts of the Secretary of Education or the Secretary of the Treasury. The duties to obtain, furnish, and publish the earnings figures are ministerial. The content of the offer is required by statute whether or not the testing is ever concluded, and where no median earnings are published no borrowing limit applies. Each provision of this Act is independently severable."},{"anchor":"sec-0-3","heading":"\u00a7 0-3. Definitions.","part":null,"body":"In this Act:\n\n**(a) \"Institution\"** means an institution of higher education that participates in a program under title IV of the Higher Education Act of 1965.\n\n**(b) \"Secretary\"** means the Secretary of Education.\n\n**(c) \"Student\"** means an individual who is enrolled at, has been enrolled at, or has applied for admission to an institution.\n\n**(d) \"Title IV\"** means title IV of the Higher Education Act of 1965 (20 U.S.C. 1070 et seq.).\n\n**(e)** A term used in an amendment made by this Act to a provision of the Higher Education Act of 1965, of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, or of title 11, United States Code, has the meaning it bears in the amended provision, and subsections (a) through (d) do not apply to it."},{"anchor":"sec-1-2","heading":"\u00a7 1-2. Amendment of section 487(a) of the Higher Education Act of 1965.","part":"TITLE I \u2014 THE CREDIT YOU PAID FOR","body":"Section 487(a) of the Higher Education Act of 1965 (20 U.S.C. \u00a7 1094(a)) is amended by inserting after paragraph (29) the following new paragraph:\n\n> **(30) (A) The institution will apply each transferred lower-division course that it has accepted for credit to any requirement of a degree or certificate program that the institution would treat as satisfied, in whole or in part, by an enrolled student's completion of a course of substantially equivalent content offered by the institution.**\n>\n> **(B) For purposes of subparagraph (A), a course is of substantially equivalent content if it covers the same subject matter at the same level. A course is not of other than substantially equivalent content by reason of its title, its credit-hour designation, its mode of delivery, the academic calendar under which it was offered, or the identity, control, or accrediting agency of the institution at which it was completed.**\n>\n> **(C) A determination by a statewide course numbering system, statewide articulation agreement, or comparable system established under the law of a State that two courses are equivalent is conclusive of substantial equivalence under subparagraph (B) as to the courses it covers.**\n>\n> **(D) The institution will deliver to the student, not later than 30 days after the earlier of the institution's receipt of the student's transcript or the student's written notice to the institution that the transcript has been sent, a written determination stating, for each transferred course accepted for credit, each requirement to which the course is applied and each requirement to which it is not. Where the institution determines that a course is not of substantially equivalent content to a course of the institution, the determination will state the specific respects in which the content differs, and will state the student's right to seek review under subparagraph (F). Not later than 5 days after receiving a student's transcript, the institution will deliver to the student a written acknowledgment stating the date of receipt. Where the institution has not delivered that acknowledgment, the transcript is treated, for purposes of this subparagraph and subparagraph (E), as received on the date on which it was transmitted to the institution, and the institution bears the burden of establishing a later date of receipt.**\n>\n> **(E) Where the institution has not delivered the written determination within the period stated in subparagraph (D), each transferred course accepted for credit is applied, by operation of this paragraph, to every requirement that a course of substantially equivalent content offered by the institution would satisfy, and no further act by any person is required.**\n>\n> **(F) The institution will provide a procedure under which a student may obtain review of a determination under subparagraph (D) by a person other than the person who made it, and will complete that review not later than 30 days after the student requests it. The availability of review under this subparagraph is not a condition of an action under section 1-3 of the College Price Before Signing Day Act.**\n>\n> **(G) The institution will not charge a fee for a determination, a review, or a transcript evaluation under this paragraph.**\n>\n> **(H) This paragraph does not require the institution to accept a transferred course for credit, does not limit a requirement that the student have attained a stated grade in the course, provided that the institution applies that requirement to its own enrolled students on the same terms, and does not limit a limitation the institution places on the total number of transferred credits it will accept or on the number of credits that must be earned at the institution.**\n>\n> **(I) The institution will publish annually, in the same place as the policies required by section 496(c)(9) of this Act, for the most recent academic year for which the institution has the data \u2014 (i) the number of students who transferred into the institution; (ii) the number of credits those students presented; (iii) the number the institution accepted for credit; and (iv) the number the institution applied to a requirement of a degree or certificate program under subparagraph (A).**\n>\n> **(J) For purposes of subparagraph (A), a transferred course is lower-division if the institution at which the course was completed designated it as a lower-division, freshman, or sophomore course, or if the course is among the first 60 semester credit hours, or the equivalent in other units, that the student completed at any institution. The receiving institution's numbering or designation of its own courses does not determine whether a transferred course is lower-division.**","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE I \u2014 THE CREDIT YOU PAID FOR","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-1-3","heading":"\u00a7 1-3. Enforcement.","part":"TITLE I \u2014 THE CREDIT YOU PAID FOR","body":"**(a) Civil action.** A student aggrieved by a violation of section 487(a)(30) of the Higher Education Act of 1965 may bring a civil action against the institution in any district court of the United States or in any court of a State of competent jurisdiction. **The jurisdiction of the courts of the States over an action under this section is concurrent with that of the district courts of the United States.**\n\n**(b) Removal barred.** Section 1445 of title 28, United States Code, is amended by adding at the end the following new subsection:\n\n> **\"(e) A civil action in any State court arising under section 1-3, 2-4, or 5-3 of the College Price Before Signing Day Act may not be removed to any district court of the United States.\"**\n\n**(c) Relief.** The court shall award to a prevailing student \u2014\n\n**(1)** the amount the student paid in tuition and fees for each course the student was required to take by reason of the violation;\n\n**(2)** an order applying the credit to each requirement to which it should have been applied; and\n\n**(3)** the costs of the action and a reasonable attorney's fee.\n\n**(d) Tax treatment.** An amount awarded under subsection (c)(1) is not includible in gross income.\n\n**(1) Treated as a refund.** For purposes of section 25A of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, the amount is treated as a refund, received in the taxable year in which it is paid, of the qualified tuition and related expenses on account of which it was awarded.\n\n**(2) Recapture.** Where the taxpayer was allowed a credit under that section for a prior taxable year determined in whole or in part by reference to those expenses, the tax imposed by chapter 1 of that Code for the taxable year in which the amount is paid is increased by the excess of the credit so allowed over the credit that would have been allowed had the expenses taken into account been reduced by the amount awarded. **This paragraph states the recapture that section 25A(j) of that Code authorizes the Secretary to prescribe by regulation, and operates whether or not such a regulation is in force.**\n\n**(e) Frivolous actions.** The court may award costs and a reasonable attorney's fee to a prevailing institution where the action was frivolous, unreasonable, or without foundation.\n\n**(f) Predispute agreements.** Notwithstanding chapter 1 of title 9, United States Code, and at the election of the student, no predispute arbitration agreement and no predispute joint-action waiver is valid or enforceable with respect to a claim under this section. **The applicability of this subsection to an agreement, and the validity and enforceability of an agreement to which this subsection applies, shall be determined by a court rather than an arbitrator, irrespective of whether the agreement purports to delegate that determination to an arbitrator and irrespective of whether the party resisting arbitration challenges the agreement specifically or in conjunction with other terms of the contract in which it appears.**\n\n**(g) Forum and choice of law.** A term of an agreement between the institution and the student that designates a forum other than a court described in subsection (a), or that designates the law of a jurisdiction other than that in which the student was enrolled or, in the case of a student who has applied for admission but has not enrolled, that in which the student resided when the application was made, is void as to a claim under this section.\n\n**(h) No assignment.** An action under this section is brought on the student's own injury. **Nothing in this section assigns to any person a claim of the United States.**"},{"anchor":"sec-1-4","heading":"\u00a7 1-4. Construction.","part":"TITLE I \u2014 THE CREDIT YOU PAID FOR","body":"**(a)** This title does not relieve an institution or an accrediting agency or association of any duty under section 496(c)(9) of the Higher Education Act of 1965.\n\n**(b)** The absence of a determination described in section 487(a)(30)(C) of that Act relieves no institution of its duty under section 487(a)(30)(A).\n\n**(c)** Nothing in this title limits a State from requiring the acceptance or application of credit on terms more protective of the student.\n\n**(d) Severability and fallback.** If section 487(a)(30)(A) or (E) of the Higher Education Act of 1965, as added by this title, is held invalid or unenforceable as applied to any institution, **subparagraphs (D), (F), (G) and (I) remain in force as to that institution**, and the institution's determination under subparagraph (D) shall state, for each course not applied to a requirement, whether the institution would treat a course of substantially equivalent content offered by the institution as satisfying that requirement. **The remainder of this title, and each other provision of it, is severable.**"},{"anchor":"sec-2-2","heading":"\u00a7 2-2. Amendment of section 487(a) of the Higher Education Act of 1965 \u2014 the financial aid offer.","part":"TITLE II \u2014 THE PRICE BEFORE THE PROMISE","body":"Section 487(a) of the Higher Education Act of 1965 (20 U.S.C. \u00a7 1094(a)) is amended by inserting after paragraph (30), as added by title I of this Act, the following new paragraph:\n\n> **(31) (A) Each financial aid offer the institution makes to a student for an award year beginning on or after the first July 1 occurring at least 24 months after the date of enactment of the College Price Before Signing Day Act will be titled \"Financial Aid Offer,\" will be stated in plain language, and will state separately and without netting one against another \u2014**\n>\n> **(i) the award year, the academic period the offer covers, and the program of study to which the offer relates;**\n>\n> **(ii) that an amount stated in the offer may change for an academic period the offer does not cover, and may differ by program;**\n>\n> **(iii) whether the amounts stated in the offer are based on full-time or on part-time enrollment;**\n>\n> **(iv) the estimated cost of attendance for that award year, distinguishing the costs the institution requires the student to pay to the institution from all other costs;**\n>\n> **(v) whether the tuition and fees stated are set for the academic period the offer covers or are an estimate, and, where they are an estimate, the basis of the estimate;**\n>\n> **(vi) each grant and each scholarship offered, by amount and by source, and whether each is renewable and on what condition;**\n>\n> **(vii) the net price for the award year, and the net price for completion, each determined under subparagraph (F);**\n>\n> **(viii) each loan offered, separately from every grant and scholarship, labeled with the word \"loan,\" distinguishing a subsidized from an unsubsidized loan, and stating for each the total amount offered, the interest rate, and every fee;**\n>\n> **(ix) each work-study opportunity offered, stated as an opportunity to earn rather than as an award, together with a statement that amounts earned are wages and are subject to tax; and**\n>\n> **(x) the address of the institution's net price calculator, and of the internet website by which the Secretary publishes institution-level and program-level data on cost, completion, debt, and post-enrollment earnings, whatever that website is then called.**\n>\n> **(B) The institution will use the same terminology for the same item in every communication with the student concerning the offer.**\n>\n> **(C) Where the institution requires a student to confirm receipt of an offer delivered electronically, the institution will not treat that confirmation as an acceptance or a rejection of the offer or of any part of it.**\n>\n> **(D) The Secretary will establish, not later than 9 months after the date of enactment of the College Price Before Signing Day Act, a process for consumer testing of the presentation of the information required by subparagraph (A), will conclude that testing not later than 8 months thereafter, will publish the resulting presentation requirements not later than 3 months after the conclusion of that testing, and will notify each institution of them. The consumer testing will include representatives of students, including low-income students, first generation college students, adult students, veterans, and servicemembers, and of their families, and of secondary school counselors, nonprofit college access organizations, lenders, and State higher education agencies.**\n>\n> **(E) The requirements published under subparagraph (D) may prescribe the form, order, and presentation of the information required by subparagraph (A), and may require additional information. They may not omit, combine, qualify, or reduce any item required by subparagraph (A). Except for the identification of the institution and of the student, nothing in the offer other than the items required by subparagraph (A), whether required by the Secretary or included by the institution, will be presented before, or more prominently than, those items. Subparagraph (A) applies according to its terms whether or not the Secretary has established the process, concluded the testing, published the requirements, or given the notice, and no act or omission of the Secretary relieves the institution of it.**\n>\n> **(F) For purposes of subparagraph (A)(vii), the net price for the award year is the estimated cost of attendance stated under subparagraph (A)(iv), reduced by the grants and scholarships stated under subparagraph (A)(vi); and the net price for completion is that amount multiplied by the number of award years in the normal time for completion of the program to which the offer relates, as stated in the institution's published description of that program. Neither figure is computed by deducting any loan or any work-study opportunity. The term \"net price\" as used in this paragraph has the meaning given in this subparagraph, and does not have the meaning given in section 132(a)(3) of this Act.**","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE II \u2014 THE PRICE BEFORE THE PROMISE","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-2-3","heading":"\u00a7 2-3. Amendment of section 487(a) \u2014 release of a binding commitment.","part":"TITLE II \u2014 THE PRICE BEFORE THE PROMISE","body":"Section 487(a) of that Act is further amended by inserting after paragraph (31), as added by section 2-2 of this Act, the following new paragraph:\n\n> **(32) (A) The institution will not enforce, and will treat as void, any commitment by an applicant for admission to enroll at the institution, or to withdraw or refrain from making an application to another institution, as against an applicant who declines admission after receiving the institution's financial aid offer.**\n>\n> **(B) The institution will not, by reason of a declination described in subparagraph (A) \u2014 (i) charge or retain any fee, deposit, or other sum; (ii) impose any condition on the applicant's later application to the institution; (iii) make any report of the declination to any other institution, association, or person; or (iv) impose any other consequence on the applicant.**\n>\n> **(C) Subparagraph (A) applies to a declination whatever the applicant's reason, and the institution will not require the applicant to state a reason, to demonstrate financial need, or to show that the offer was insufficient.**\n>\n> **(D) Not later than the date the institution communicates a decision on an application made under a program requiring a commitment described in subparagraph (A), the institution will deliver to the applicant the financial aid offer for the award year to which the application relates, and will state in that communication, in the same document, that the applicant may decline and that no consequence described in subparagraph (B) may follow.**\n>\n> **(E) This paragraph does not prohibit the institution from offering admission on a schedule of its choosing, from requiring an applicant to apply to the institution alone before a stated date, or from requiring a deposit from an applicant who accepts.**\n>\n> **(F) Where the applicant has not provided the institution with information the institution requires to compute an item of the offer, the institution satisfies subparagraph (D) by delivering the offer computed from the information the applicant has provided, identifying each item it cannot compute and the information it lacks. A commitment described in subparagraph (A) to enroll at the institution is not enforceable as against an applicant to whom the institution has not delivered an offer under subparagraph (D) or this subparagraph.**\n>"},{"anchor":"sec-2-4","heading":"\u00a7 2-4. Enforcement.","part":"TITLE II \u2014 THE PRICE BEFORE THE PROMISE","body":"**(a) Civil action.** Subsections (a), (b), (e), (f), (g), and (h) of section 1-3 of this Act apply to a violation of paragraph (31) or (32) of section 487(a) of the Higher Education Act of 1965 as they apply to a violation of paragraph (30) of that section.\n\n**(b) Relief.** The court shall award to a prevailing student \u2014\n\n**(1)** where the violation is of paragraph (31), an order directing the institution to deliver to the student an offer conforming to that paragraph;\n\n**(2)** where the violation is of paragraph (32), an order declaring void each commitment described in paragraph (32)(A) and enjoining each consequence described in paragraph (32)(B);\n\n**(3)** the amount of any fee, deposit, or other sum charged or retained in violation of paragraph (32)(B)(i); and\n\n**(4)** the costs of the action and a reasonable attorney's fee.\n\n**(c)** A student who receives an offer that omits an item required by paragraph (31)(A) is injured by the omission for purposes of this section."},{"anchor":"sec-2-5","heading":"\u00a7 2-5. Construction and effective dates.","part":"TITLE II \u2014 THE PRICE BEFORE THE PROMISE","body":"**(a)** Paragraph (31) applies to a financial aid offer for an award year beginning on or after the first July 1 occurring at least 24 months after the date of enactment of this Act. **Paragraph (32) applies to a commitment sought or taken on or after the date of enactment of this Act**, and does not depend on paragraph (31) having taken effect.\n\n**(b) Severability and fallback.** If paragraph (32)(A) is held invalid or unenforceable as applied to any institution, **paragraphs (32)(B), (C) and (D) remain in force as to that institution**. The remainder of this title, and each provision of it, is severable."},{"anchor":"sec-3-2","heading":"\u00a7 3-2. Repeal of the eligibility consequence.","part":"TITLE III \u2014 A LOAN YOU CAN REPAY","body":"**(a) THE FOUR LIMBS OF SECTION 84001.** Section 454 of the Higher Education Act of 1965 (20 U.S.C. 1087d) is amended\u2014\n\n**(1)** in subsection (a), by striking paragraph (6) and redesignating paragraph (7) as paragraph (6);\n\n**(2)** in subsection (b)(2), by striking `\"(6), and (7)\"` and inserting `\"and (6)\"`;\n\n**(3)** by striking subsection (c); and\n\n**(4)** by redesignating subsection (d) as subsection (c).\n\n**(b) NO ELIGIBILITY CONSEQUENCE FROM THE EARNINGS OF COMPLETERS.\u2014**\n\n**(1) IN GENERAL.** No program's eligibility to participate in any program under title IV of the Higher Education Act of 1965 shall be determined by reference to the earnings of its completers.\n\n**(2) WHATEVER AUTHORITY IS INVOKED.** Paragraph (1) applies to any determination of the eligibility of an institution or a program to participate in any program under title IV of that Act, however reached \u2014 by regulation, by program participation agreement, by certification or recertification, by termination or revocation of program eligibility, or by construction of any condition of eligibility under that title, including the condition that a program prepare students for gainful employment in a recognized occupation. **A regulation, agreement, or determination inconsistent with paragraph (1) has no force.**\n\n**(3) WHAT THIS SUBSECTION DOES NOT REACH.** Nothing in this subsection limits any requirement that the Secretary or an institution compute, publish, disclose, or obtain a student's acknowledgment of a program's earnings, debt, debt-to-earnings rates, or earnings premium. **Those requirements continue in force, and none of them is conditioned on the figures taking any value.**","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE III \u2014 A LOAN YOU CAN REPAY","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-3-3","heading":"\u00a7 3-3. The borrowing limit.","part":"TITLE III \u2014 A LOAN YOU CAN REPAY","body":"Part D of title IV of the Higher Education Act of 1965 (20 U.S.C. 1087a et seq.) is amended by adding at the end the following new section:\n\n> **\"SEC. 461. PROGRAM BORROWING LIMIT.**\n>\n> **\"(a) LIMIT.\u2014 The aggregate amount a student may borrow under this part for enrollment in a program of study may not exceed the program's borrowing limit, determined under subsection (b). The limit under this section applies in addition to, and not in lieu of, any other limit on borrowing under this part, and where both apply the lesser controls.**\n>\n> **\"(b) DETERMINATION.\u2014 A program's borrowing limit is the principal amount whose amortization in level monthly installments, over the applicable period under subsection (d) and at the interest rate then applicable under section 455(b) to a Federal Direct Unsubsidized Stafford Loan issued to an undergraduate student, in the case of a program leading to a credential below the master's level, or to such a loan issued to a graduate or professional student, in the case of any other program, produces annual payments equal to the program's annual service allowance under subsection (c).**\n>\n> **\"(c) ANNUAL SERVICE ALLOWANCE.\u2014 A program's annual service allowance is the greater of\u2014**\n>\n> **\"(1) 20 percent of the amount by which the program's median earnings exceed 150 percent of the poverty line for a single individual, as most recently published; or**\n>\n> **\"(2) 8 percent of the program's median earnings.**\n>\n> **\"(d) APPLICABLE PERIOD.\u2014 The applicable period is\u2014**\n>\n> **\"(1) 10 years, for a program leading to a credential below the master's level;**\n>\n> **\"(2) 15 years, for a program leading to a master's degree; and**\n>\n> **\"(3) 25 years, for a program leading to a doctoral degree or a first-professional degree.**\n>\n> **\"(e) PROGRAM, AND MEDIAN EARNINGS.\u2014**\n>\n> **\"(1) PROGRAM.\u2014 For purposes of this section, a program of study is an eligible program, within the meaning of section 481(b), identified by the combination of the credential level and the classification of instructional programs code at which the institution reports it to the Secretary.**\n>\n> **\"(2) MEDIAN EARNINGS.\u2014 A program's median earnings are the median annual earnings of the students who completed the program, identified from the data the Secretary already obtains for purposes of this title, measured 4 years after completion, furnished by the Secretary of the Treasury under paragraph (6), excluding a student who during the year of measurement was enrolled full time in another eligible program. The Secretary shall determine the cohort, any exclusion from it, the items of return information that constitute annual earnings, the attribution of any such item reported on a joint return, and the treatment of a completer for whom no such item is found, by a single methodology applied to every program at every institution, and shall publish that methodology, and any change in it, not later than the date of the first request under paragraph (6)(A) to which it applies. The Secretary of the Treasury shall compute the figures under paragraph (6) by that methodology. The Secretary shall pool the completers of up to 4 consecutive years to reach a cohort of 30 or more completers.**\n>\n> **\"(3) SMALL COHORTS.\u2014 Where fewer than 30 students completed a program in the pooled period, the program's median earnings are the median annual earnings, determined under paragraph (2) over the same pooled period, of the students who completed any program at the institution at the same credential level and in the same four-digit classification of instructional programs code. Where that group also has fewer than 30 completers, the program's median earnings are the median annual earnings, so determined, of the students who completed any program at the institution at the same credential level.**\n>\n> **\"(4) A PROGRAM REPORTED ANEW.\u2014 Where an institution reports a program at a credential level or classification of instructional programs code other than that at which it reported the program's predecessor, the institution shall identify the predecessor to the Secretary when it first reports the program, and until 30 or more students have completed the program under its new reporting the program's median earnings are determined under paragraphs (2) and (3) from the completers of the program and of its predecessor together.**\n>\n> **\"(5) NO INCREASE ON A NARROWER COHORT.\u2014 For any year in which the share of the completers from whom a program's median earnings are determined who received a Federal Pell Grant at any time is less than that share among the completers from whom the program's borrowing limit was first determined, the program's median earnings for that year are treated, for purposes of subsections (b) and (c), as not exceeding its median earnings for the preceding year. Each share is determined from the same completers, pooled in the same manner, as the median earnings to which it relates. The Secretary of the Treasury shall compute each share from the Pell status stated for each completer in the list supplied by the Secretary under paragraph (6)(A). Where a program's median earnings are determined under paragraph (4) from the completers of a predecessor, the share among the completers from whom the predecessor's borrowing limit was first determined is the share against which the program is compared.**\n>\n> **\"(6) OBTAINING AND FURNISHING THE FIGURES.\u2014**\n>\n> **\"(A) REQUEST.\u2014 The Secretary shall obtain from the Secretary of the Treasury the median earnings required under paragraphs (2) through (4) and the shares required under paragraph (5). The Secretary shall make a written request for those figures not later than January 1 preceding the first July 1 occurring at least 18 months after the date of enactment of the College Price Before Signing Day Act, and not later than January 1 of each year thereafter. Not later than each date by which a request is required under this subparagraph, the Secretary shall publish the date on which the request was made and the number of completers listed for each cohort and pooled group. Each request shall include a list of the completers for each cohort and pooled group, determined under paragraphs (2) through (4), with the information necessary to identify each completer and match the completer to return information, the applicable year of measurement, and whether the completer received a Federal Pell Grant at any time. The most recent year of measurement in a request is the most recent calendar year that ended not less than 12 months before the date of the request. The duty to obtain the figures and to make and supply the request and list is ministerial and is not committed to the discretion of the Secretary. Neither section 134 of this Act nor section 552a(b) of title 5, United States Code, bars the Secretary from compiling the list or supplying it to the Secretary of the Treasury under this subparagraph. The Secretary of the Treasury shall use a list supplied under this subparagraph only to compute the figures required by subparagraph (B), shall not disclose the list or any information derived from it other than those figures and a statement under subparagraph (D), and shall not retain the list after furnishing those figures.**\n>\n> **\"(B) FURNISHING.\u2014 Upon request of the Secretary under subparagraph (A), the Secretary of the Treasury shall compute and furnish to the Secretary, as a special statistical study under section 6108(b) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, the median earnings required under paragraphs (2) through (4) and the shares required under paragraph (5), not later than the expiration of the period specified in item S-1 of the SCHEDULE to the College Price Before Signing Day Act after the date on which the request and list are transmitted to the Secretary of the Treasury, and in any case not later than 30 days before the date by which the figures must be published under subsection (h). A list is sufficient for purposes of this subparagraph unless, not later than 30 days after its transmission, the Secretary of the Treasury identifies in writing each completer for whom the list lacks the information necessary to match the completer to return information, and the figures shall be computed within that period from every other completer whether or not the list is supplemented. The duty to compute and furnish those figures is ministerial and is not committed to the discretion of the Secretary of the Treasury. Not later than the expiration of the period that applies under this subparagraph, the Secretary of the Treasury shall publish the date on which the figures were furnished.**\n>\n> **\"(C) NO FEE.\u2014 The sentence of section 6108(b) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 authorizing a reasonable fee for the cost of the work or services performed does not apply to the special statistical study required by subparagraph (B), and that study shall be performed and furnished without fee.**\n>\n> **\"(D) DISCLOSURE AVOIDANCE.\u2014 Where the Secretary of the Treasury determines that section 6108(c) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 bars furnishing a figure required by subparagraph (B) in the form computed, the Secretary of the Treasury shall furnish the figure rounded or adjusted by a disclosure-avoidance method that is published and applied uniformly to every figure under this paragraph, and the figure so furnished is treated as the figure required by subparagraph (B). Where no such method permits a figure to be furnished, the Secretary of the Treasury shall, within the period that applies under subparagraph (B), furnish to the Secretary a written statement identifying the program or group and the figure withheld and stating the respect in which furnishing it would permit a particular taxpayer to be identified, and the Secretary shall publish the statement with the figures published under subsection (h).**\n>\n> **\"(f) WHERE NO FIGURE EXISTS.\u2014 Where the Secretary has not published a program's median earnings, including where fewer than 30 students completed any program at the institution at the program's credential level in the pooled period, no limit applies under this section to that program. The absence of a published figure shall not reduce any student's eligibility to borrow.**\n>\n> **\"(g) NO OTHER CONSEQUENCE.\u2014 A program's borrowing limit, and the figures from which it is derived, shall not be used to determine the eligibility of any institution or program to participate in any program under this title, and no determination under this title other than the limit itself shall be made by reference to them.\"**"},{"anchor":"sec-3-4","heading":"\u00a7 3-4. Publication.","part":"TITLE III \u2014 A LOAN YOU CAN REPAY","body":"**(a)** Section 461 of that Act, as added by section 3-3 of this Act, is amended by adding at the end the following new subsection:\n\n> **\"(h) PUBLICATION.\u2014 For every program of study at every participating institution, whether or not a limit applies, the Secretary shall publish annually, not later than July 1, beginning with the first July 1 occurring at least 18 months after the date of enactment of the College Price Before Signing Day Act, the program's median earnings, its applicable period, its annual service allowance, and its borrowing limit; where the median earnings are determined under subsection (e)(3) or (e)(4), a statement that they are pooled and the group of programs from which they are determined; and the two shares compared under subsection (e)(5), whether or not paragraph (5) applies. The institution shall publish the same figures for its own programs, not later than 30 days after the Secretary publishes them or any correction of them, in the place at which it publishes the disclosures required by section 485. Publication under this subsection is not conditioned on the program's figures taking any value. The duty to publish under this subsection is ministerial, and is not committed to the discretion of the Secretary or of the institution.\"**\n\n**(b) Action to compel collection and publication.** A student who has sought a figure required to be published under subsection (h), or a date required to be published under subsection (e)(6), of section 461 of that Act and has not obtained it is injured by the denial, which deprives the student of information bearing on the student's decision whether to enroll in, continue in, or borrow for a program of study, and may bring a civil action to compel publication or performance of a duty under subsection (e)(6) of that section \u2014\n\n**(1)** against the Secretary or the Secretary of the Treasury, as to the duty imposed on that officer, in the United States District Court for the District of Columbia or in the district court of the United States for the district in which the student resides; or\n\n**(2)** against the institution, in a court described in section 1-3(a) of this Act, to which subsections (b), (e), (f), and (g) of section 1-3 of this Act apply.\n\n**(c) Relief.** The court shall award to a prevailing plaintiff an order directing publication of each figure or date not published and, where a figure has not been furnished under section 461(e)(6)(B) of that Act, performance of the duties under section 461(e)(6) of that Act, and the costs of the action and a reasonable attorney's fee. A plaintiff is a prevailing plaintiff for purposes of the award of costs and fees under this subsection where the plaintiff obtains relief through a judicial order, an enforceable written agreement or consent decree, or a voluntary or unilateral change in position by the defendant after the action is brought, if the plaintiff's claim is not insubstantial. The furnishing or publication of a figure or date after an action under this section is brought does not render moot a claim for relief as to the duty in a later year, and a defendant asserting mootness bears the burden of establishing that the failure cannot reasonably be expected to recur. Section 1082(a)(2) of title 20, United States Code, does not bar an order under this subsection."},{"anchor":"sec-3-5","heading":"\u00a7 3-5. The offer, and the acknowledgment that waives nothing.","part":"TITLE III \u2014 A LOAN YOU CAN REPAY","body":"Section 461 of that Act, as added by section 3-3 of this Act and amended by section 3-4 of this Act, is further amended by adding at the end the following new subsection:\n\n> **\"(i) WHERE THE LIMIT BINDS.\u2014**\n>\n> **\"(1) Where a program's borrowing limit is less than the amount a student would otherwise be eligible to borrow for that program, the institution shall state in the financial aid offer required by section 487(a)(31), beside the amount the student is asked to borrow \u2014 (A) the program's borrowing limit, its median earnings, and its annual service allowance; and (B) the same three figures for every other program at the institution in the same two-digit classification of instructional programs, and the Secretary's published figures for that classification nationally.**\n>\n> **\"(2) The institution shall obtain the student's acknowledgment of receipt of the statement required by paragraph (1) before the student's first disbursement, and may obtain it together with any other acknowledgment the institution collects under this title, so long as the statement required by paragraph (1) is displayed separately. The acknowledgment is not a condition of enrollment, of registration, or of the institution's entry into an enrollment agreement.**\n>\n> **\"(3) An acknowledgment under paragraph (2) is not evidence of informed consent, waives no claim, is not a defense in any action, and shall not be treated as the student's agreement to any term.\"**"},{"anchor":"sec-3-6","heading":"\u00a7 3-6. Construction, severability, and effective dates.","part":"TITLE III \u2014 A LOAN YOU CAN REPAY","body":"**(a)** Nothing in this title limits the tuition, fees, or other charges an institution may impose, or requires any program to change what it teaches or whom it admits.\n\n**(b)** Nothing in this title limits a student's eligibility for a grant under title IV.\n\n**(c) Effective dates.** Section 3-2 takes effect on the date of enactment. The section added by section 3-3 applies to loans first disbursed for a period of enrollment beginning on or after the first July 1 occurring at least 18 months after the date of enactment.\n\n**(d) Severability and fallback.** Each provision of this title is severable. **If subsection (a) of the section added by section 3-3 is held invalid or unenforceable as applied to any institution, subsections (b) through (i) remain in force as to that institution**."},{"anchor":"sec-4-2","heading":"\u00a7 4-2. Amendment of section 484B of the Higher Education Act of 1965.","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 WHEN YOU LEAVE, YOU STOP PAYING","body":"**(a) The cliff struck.** Section 484B(a)(3)(B) of the Higher Education Act of 1965 (20 U.S.C. \u00a7 1091b(a)(3)(B)) is amended to read as follows:\n\n> **\"(B) PERCENTAGE EARNED.\u2014 For purposes of subparagraph (A)(i), the percentage of grant or loan assistance under this title that has been earned by the student is equal to the percentage of the payment period or period of enrollment for which assistance was awarded that was completed (as determined in accordance with subsection (d)) as of the day the student withdrew.\"**\n\n**Subparagraph (B) as it now reads, struck in its entirety:**\n\n> ~~For purposes of subparagraph (A)(i), the percentage of grant or loan assistance under this title that has been earned by the student is\u2014~~\n>\n> ~~(i) equal to the percentage of the payment period or period of enrollment for which assistance was awarded that was completed (as determined in accordance with subsection (d)) as of the day the student withdrew, provided that such date occurs on or before the completion of 60 percent of the payment period or period of enrollment; or~~\n>\n> ~~(ii) 100 percent, if the day the student withdrew occurs after the student has completed (as determined in accordance with subsection (d)) 60 percent of the payment period or period of enrollment.~~\n\n**(b) Continuous, and stated to be.** Section 484B(a)(3) of that Act is further amended by adding at the end the following:\n\n> **\"(D) The percentage determined under subparagraph (B) is a continuous function of the portion of the payment period or period of enrollment completed. No portion of the assistance becomes earned by reason of the student's having completed any stated proportion of that period.\"**\n\n**(c) The student returns no more than before.** Section 484B(b) of that Act is amended by adding at the end the following:\n\n> **\"(4) LIMITATION ON STUDENT GRANT RETURN.\u2014 Notwithstanding paragraph (2), a student shall not be required to return grant assistance under that paragraph, for a payment period or period of enrollment, in an amount greater than the grant assistance the student would have been required to return under that paragraph had paragraph (3) of subsection (a) and subsection (c)(1)(A)(iii), each as in effect on the day before the date of enactment of the College Price Before Signing Day Act, applied. For purposes of this paragraph, where the day the student withdrew is determined under subsection (c)(1)(A)(iii), it is the later of the mid-point of the payment period for which assistance under this title was disbursed and the day determined under that clause as amended by that Act. Each amount in the computation of the grant assistance the student would have been required to return, including the amount the institution would have been required to return under paragraph (1), is determined as though those provisions as so in effect had applied. This paragraph does not increase the amount the institution is required to return under paragraph (1).\"**\n\n**(d) The day the student withdrew.** Section 484B(c)(1)(A)(iii) of that Act (20 U.S.C. \u00a7 1091b(c)(1)(A)(iii)) is amended to read as follows, new language marked as inserted and struck language marked:\n\n> \"(iii) in the case of a student who does not begin the withdrawal process or otherwise notify the institution of the intent to withdraw, **the last date of the student's attendance at an academically related activity that the institution's records establish or, where the institution's records establish no such date,** the date that is the mid-point of the payment period for which assistance under this title was disbursed ~~or a later date documented by the institution~~; or\"","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE IV \u2014 WHEN YOU LEAVE, YOU STOP PAYING","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-4-3","heading":"\u00a7 4-3. Construction.","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 WHEN YOU LEAVE, YOU STOP PAYING","body":"**(a)** Amounts returned under section 484B of the Higher Education Act of 1965 by reason of this title are returned to the programs under title IV, and a return of loan funds reduces the borrower's obligation on the loan to the extent returned. **This title imposes no new obligation on a student.**\n\n**(b)** This title does not regulate the tuition, fees, or other charges an institution may impose, and does not require an institution to refund any institutional charge. **It governs only the assistance provided under title IV.**\n\n**(c)** Nothing in this title affects the treatment of a student who completes the payment period or period of enrollment.\n\n**(d) Effective date.** This title applies to a payment period or period of enrollment beginning on or after the first July 1 occurring at least 180 days after the date of enactment of this Act.\n\n**(e) Severability.** Each provision of this title is severable."},{"anchor":"sec-5-2","heading":"\u00a7 5-2. Amendment of section 487(a) of the Higher Education Act of 1965.","part":"TITLE V \u2014 ONE SET OF NUMBERS","body":"Section 487(a) of the Higher Education Act of 1965 (20 U.S.C. \u00a7 1094(a)) is amended by inserting after paragraph (32), as added by title II of this Act, the following new paragraph:\n\n> **(33) (A) Where the institution supplies to any person who publishes a ranking, rating, score, or comparative assessment of institutions of higher education a figure that corresponds to a figure the institution reports under paragraph (17), the figure supplied will equal the figure reported.**\n>\n> **(B) A figure corresponds to a reported figure if it purports to measure the same thing about the institution for the same period, whatever it is called and however it is expressed, and whether it is supplied as a number, a range, a rank, a category, or a narrative statement.**\n>\n> **(C) Where the institution has not yet reported a figure under paragraph (17) for the period to which a supplied figure relates, the institution will report that figure under paragraph (17) as it supplied it, unless before the report is due the institution has supplied the corrected figure to each person to whom it supplied the original and has published the correction under subparagraph (D), in which case the institution will report the corrected figure. This subparagraph does not relieve the institution of any requirement of paragraph (17) as to the definitions or methodology on which a reported figure is computed.**\n>\n> **(D) The institution will publish, in the place at which it publishes the disclosures required by section 485 of this Act, and within 30 days after supplying them, every figure it supplies to a person described in subparagraph (A), identifying the person and the period.**\n>\n> **(E) Nothing in this paragraph requires the institution to supply any figure to any person, or limits the institution's decision not to.**\n>\n> **(F) This paragraph imposes no duty on, and creates no cause of action against, any person who publishes a ranking, rating, score, or comparative assessment.**","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE V \u2014 ONE SET OF NUMBERS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-5-3","heading":"\u00a7 5-3. Enforcement.","part":"TITLE V \u2014 ONE SET OF NUMBERS","body":"**(a)** A violation of paragraph (33) of section 487(a) of the Higher Education Act of 1965 is a violation of the program participation agreement and bears on the administrative capability of the institution within the meaning of paragraph (3) of that subsection. **A violation bears on administrative capability under this subsection only where the institution \u2014**\n\n**(1)** supplied a figure to a person described in subparagraph (A) of paragraph (33) knowing, or with reckless disregard of whether, the figure differed from the figure reported under paragraph (17);\n\n**(2)** reported under paragraph (17) a figure different from the figure it supplied, where subparagraph (C) of paragraph (33) required it to report the figure as supplied; or\n\n**(3)** failed to publish a figure as required by subparagraph (D) of paragraph (33) after written notice of the failure and a period of not less than 30 days in which to cure it.\n\n**(b) Civil action.** Subsections (a), (b), and (e) through (h) of section 1-3 of this Act apply to a violation of subparagraph (D) of that paragraph as they apply to a violation of paragraph (30) of that section, except that an action for a violation of subparagraph (D) may be brought by any individual who has sought a publication required by that subparagraph and has not obtained it, whether or not that individual is a student.\n\n**(c) Injury.** An individual who has sought a publication required by subparagraph (D) and has not obtained it is injured by the denial for purposes of this section.\n\n**(d) Relief.** The court shall award to a prevailing plaintiff an order directing the institution to publish under subparagraph (D) each figure it has not published, and the costs of the action and a reasonable attorney's fee."},{"anchor":"sec-5-4","heading":"\u00a7 5-4. Construction.","part":"TITLE V \u2014 ONE SET OF NUMBERS","body":"**(a)** Nothing in this title regulates the content, methodology, or publication of any ranking, rating, score, or comparative assessment, or the person who publishes it.\n\n**(b)** Nothing in this title requires an institution to participate in any ranking, rating, score, or comparative assessment.\n\n**(c) Severability.** Each provision of this title is severable. **If subparagraph (A) of paragraph (33) is held invalid or unenforceable as applied to any institution, subparagraph (D) remains in force as to that institution**."},{"anchor":"sec-6-2","heading":"\u00a7 6-2. Amendment of section 132 of the Higher Education Act of 1965.","part":"TITLE VI \u2014 WHAT THE PRICE BUYS","body":"Section 132 of the Higher Education Act of 1965 (20 U.S.C. \u00a7 1015a) is amended by adding at the end the following new subsection:\n\n> **\"(m) WHAT THE PRICE PAYS FOR.\u2014**\n>\n> **\"(1) PUBLICATION.\u2014 Each institution shall publish, and shall display together with any estimate produced by its net price calculator under subsection (h), for the most recent year for which the institution has reported them \u2014 (A) its expenditures on instruction per full-time equivalent student; (B) its expenditures on auxiliary enterprises and on student services per full-time equivalent student, stated separately; (C) its expenditures on academic support and on institutional support per full-time equivalent student, stated separately; and (D) its total expenses and deductions per full-time equivalent student.**\n>\n> **\"(2) SOURCE OF THE FIGURES.\u2014 The figures published under paragraph (1) shall be those the institution reports under section 487(a)(17) of this Act, computed on the same definitions, and shall not be recomputed, combined, or adjusted for publication under this subsection. For purposes of paragraph (1), 'full-time equivalent student' has the meaning used by the institution in reporting under section 487(a)(17).**\n>\n> **\"(3) NO OTHER REQUIREMENT.\u2014 Nothing in this subsection limits, conditions, or establishes any standard for an institution's expenditures, the ratio between them, or their allocation, and no determination under any provision of this Act shall be made by reference to the figures published under this subsection.\"**","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE VI \u2014 WHAT THE PRICE BUYS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-6-3","heading":"\u00a7 6-3. Construction.","part":"TITLE VI \u2014 WHAT THE PRICE BUYS","body":"**(a)** This title requires publication and nothing else. **It establishes no expenditure standard, no ratio, no cap, and no floor, and creates no eligibility consequence.**\n\n**(b)** Nothing in this title requires an institution to alter how it reports under section 487(a)(17).\n\n**(c) Severability.** Each provision of this title is severable."},{"anchor":"sec-7-2","heading":"\u00a7 7-2. Amendment of section 132 of the Higher Education Act of 1965 \u2014 what net price means.","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 THE ENDOWMENT TAX BUYS A LOWER PRICE","body":"**(a)** Section 132(a)(3) of the Higher Education Act of 1965 (20 U.S.C. \u00a7 1015a(a)(3)) is amended to read as follows:\n\n> **\"(3) NET PRICE.\u2014 The term 'net price' means the average yearly amount actually paid, by or on behalf of degree- or certificate-seeking undergraduate students enrolled at an institution, for tuition, required fees, and other charges imposed by the institution, after deducting all grant and scholarship aid from any source. The term shall be computed and reported separately for each income category described in subsection (i)(6), and in the aggregate.\"**\n\n**Paragraph (3) as it now reads, struck in its entirety:**\n\n> ~~The term \"net price\" means the average yearly price actually charged to first-time, full-time undergraduate students receiving student aid at an institution of higher education after deducting such aid, which shall be determined by calculating the difference between\u2014~~\n>\n> ~~(A) the institution's cost of attendance for the year for which the determination is made; and~~\n>\n> ~~(B) the quotient of\u2014~~\n>\n> ~~(i) the total amount of need-based grant aid and merit-based grant aid, from Federal, State, and institutional sources, provided to such students enrolled in the institution for such year; and~~\n>\n> ~~(ii) the total number of such students receiving such need-based grant aid or merit-based grant aid for such year.~~\n\n**(b)** Section 132 of that Act is further amended by adding at the end, after subsection (m) as added by title VI of this Act, the following new subsection:\n\n> **\"(n) VERIFICATION.\u2014 Each institution shall have the net price it reports under this section for a year examined by an independent public accountant, who shall state whether it is computed in accordance with subsection (a)(3). An institution shall not report a net price derived from a rate of discount against a price the institution sets, in place of amounts actually paid.\"**\n\n**(c) The income categories regain their 2008 value.** Section 132(i)(6) of that Act is amended by striking subparagraph (B) and inserting the following:\n\n> **\"(B) ADJUSTMENT.\u2014 For each academic year, each of the dollar amounts at which the categories in clauses (i) through (iv) of subparagraph (A) end is increased by the percentage, if any, by which the Consumer Price Index for All Urban Consumers for the calendar year preceding the calendar year in which the academic year begins exceeds that index for calendar year 2008, rounding each resulting amount to the nearest $1,000. Each adjustment is made to the amounts stated in subparagraph (A), and not to an amount adjusted for an earlier year. Each category after the first begins at $1 more than the amount at which the category before it ends. In this subparagraph and subparagraph (C), the Consumer Price Index for All Urban Consumers for a calendar year is its average for that year as published by the Bureau of Labor Statistics, and an academic year is treated as beginning on July 1 of the award year, as defined in section 481(a)(1), in which it begins. The Secretary shall publish the adjusted categories before the academic year begins. The amounts determined under this subparagraph and subparagraph (C) apply by operation of law whether or not the Secretary has published them, and the duty to publish them is ministerial. The categories as so adjusted, with the amount set under subparagraph (C), apply for reporting under this subsection and for every purpose for which any provision of law refers to the income categories described in subparagraph (A).**\n>\n> **\"(C) BOUNDARY AT THE MEDIAN.\u2014**\n>\n> **\"(i) FIRST YEAR.\u2014 For the first academic year to which this subparagraph applies, the amount at which a category ends, as adjusted under subparagraph (B) for that year, that is nearest the national median family income, or the lower of two amounts equally near it, is replaced with the national median family income, rounded to the nearest $1,000.**\n>\n> **\"(ii) LATER YEARS.\u2014 For each later academic year, the amount replaced under clause (i) shall be the amount set under clause (i), increased by the percentage, if any, by which the Consumer Price Index for All Urban Consumers for the calendar year preceding the calendar year in which that academic year begins exceeds that index for the calendar year preceding the calendar year in which the first academic year under clause (i) begins, rounded to the nearest $1,000, and not the amount subparagraph (B) would produce.**\n>\n> **\"(iii) NATIONAL MEDIAN FAMILY INCOME.\u2014 For purposes of this subparagraph, the national median family income is the median family income for the United States for the most recent calendar year for which the Bureau of the Census has both calculated and reported it before the first academic year under clause (i) begins, increased by the percentage, if any, by which the Consumer Price Index for All Urban Consumers for the calendar year preceding the calendar year in which that academic year begins exceeds that index for that most recent calendar year.\"**\n\n**Subparagraph (B) as it now reads, struck in its entirety:**\n\n> ~~The Secretary may adjust the income categories listed in subparagraph (A) using the Consumer Price Index if the Secretary determines such adjustment is necessary.~~","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE VII \u2014 THE ENDOWMENT TAX BUYS A LOWER PRICE","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-7-3","heading":"\u00a7 7-3. Amendment of section 4968 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 \u2014 the rate falls when the price falls.","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 THE ENDOWMENT TAX BUYS A LOWER PRICE","body":"Section 4968 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 is amended by adding at the end the following new subsection:\n\n> **\"(i) REDUCTION FOR REDUCTION IN NET PRICE.\u2014**\n>\n> **\"(1) IN GENERAL.\u2014 The rate of tax otherwise applicable under subsection (b) for a taxable year shall be reduced by one percentage point for each 4 percent by which the institution's qualified net price for that year is less than its base net price, and shall not be reduced below zero. The reduction is continuous and is not determined in steps: a difference of less than 4 percent, and a difference that is not a whole multiple of 4 percent, each produces a proportionate reduction.**\n>\n> **\"(2) QUALIFIED NET PRICE.\u2014 The term 'qualified net price' means the average of the net prices, as defined in section 132(a)(3) of the Higher Education Act of 1965 and verified under section 132(n) of that Act, reported for the qualifying income categories, weighted by the number of students the institution reports in each of those categories. A qualifying income category is an income category described in section 132(i)(6) of that Act, as adjusted under section 132(i)(6)(B) and (C) of that Act, the upper dollar amount of which, for the first academic year to which section 132(i)(6)(C) of that Act applies, does not exceed the amount set under section 132(i)(6)(C)(i) of that Act; an income category that states no upper dollar amount is not a qualifying income category. The categories that qualify for that academic year are the qualifying income categories for every taxable year, whatever their adjusted amounts in a later year. The qualified net price for a taxable year is the figure so determined for the academic year beginning in that taxable year.**\n>\n> **\"(3) BASE NET PRICE.\u2014 The term 'base net price' means the institution's qualified net price for the base year, increased for each subsequent taxable year by the percentage increase in the Consumer Price Index for All Urban Consumers. The base year is the taxable year in which begins the first academic year to which the amendments made by section 7-2 of the College Price Before Signing Day Act apply. No reduction is allowed under this subsection for the base year or for any earlier taxable year. The base net price shall not be redetermined by reason of any reduction under this subsection.**\n>\n> **\"(4) ENROLLMENT CONDITION.\u2014 No reduction is allowed under this subsection for a taxable year in which the number of students in the qualifying income categories enrolled at the institution is less than that number for the taxable year used to determine base net price under paragraph (3), increased or decreased in proportion to the change in the institution's total undergraduate enrollment since that year. The qualifying income categories are those determined under paragraph (2), as adjusted under section 132(i)(6)(B) and (C) of the Higher Education Act of 1965, so that the number for a taxable year and the number for the base year are counted in constant dollars.**\n>\n> **\"(5) NO INCREASE.\u2014 Nothing in this subsection increases the rate of tax otherwise applicable under subsection (b), and no amount is recaptured under this subsection by reason of a later increase in qualified net price.**\n>\n> **\"(6) FAILURE TO REPORT.\u2014 An institution that does not report a verified net price for a year has no reduction under this subsection for that year.\"**"},{"anchor":"sec-7-4","heading":"\u00a7 7-4. Construction and effective dates.","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 THE ENDOWMENT TAX BUYS A LOWER PRICE","body":"**(a)** The amendments made by section 7-2 apply to reporting for academic years beginning on or after the first July 1 occurring at least 18 months after the date of enactment of this Act. The amendment made by section 7-3 applies to taxable years beginning after the base year determined under section 4968(i)(3) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, as added by section 7-3.\n\n**(b)** Nothing in this title limits the tuition, fees, or other charges an institution may impose.\n\n**(c) Severability.** Each provision of this title is severable. **If the amendment made by section 7-3 is held invalid or unenforceable, the amendments made by section 7-2 remain in force**.\n\n**(d) Reach of the income-categorized figures.** The aggregate net price under section 132(a)(3) of the Higher Education Act of 1965, as amended by section 7-2, reaches every degree- or certificate-seeking undergraduate student. The figures computed by income category under that section reach only the students to whom section 132(i)(6) of that Act applies, who are students receiving Federal student financial aid under title IV of that Act, and the reduction under section 4968(i) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 is measured on those figures."},{"anchor":"sec-8-2","heading":"\u00a7 8-2. Amendment of section 523(a)(8) of title 11, United States Code.","part":"TITLE VIII \u2014 A PRIVATE LOAN ANSWERS TO A LENDER'S JUDGMENT","body":"Section 523(a)(8) of title 11, United States Code, is amended \u2014\n\n**(1)** in subparagraph (A)(ii), by striking \"**; or**\" and inserting \"**;**\"; and\n\n**(2)** by striking subparagraph (B).\n\n**As amended, section 523(a)(8) will read:**\n\n> (8) unless excepting such debt from discharge under this paragraph would impose an undue hardship on the debtor and the debtor's dependents, for\u2014\n>\n> (A)(i) an educational benefit overpayment or loan made, insured, or guaranteed by a governmental unit, or made under any program funded in whole or in part by a governmental unit or nonprofit institution; or\n>\n> (ii) an obligation to repay funds received as an educational benefit, scholarship, or stipend**;**\n>\n> ~~(B) any other educational loan that is a qualified education loan, as defined in section 221(d)(1) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, incurred by a debtor who is an individual;~~","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE VIII \u2014 A PRIVATE LOAN ANSWERS TO A LENDER'S JUDGMENT","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-8-4","heading":"\u00a7 8-4. Amendment of section 707(b) of title 11, United States Code.","part":"TITLE VIII \u2014 A PRIVATE LOAN ANSWERS TO A LENDER'S JUDGMENT","body":"Section 707(b) of title 11, United States Code, is amended by adding at the end the following new paragraph:\n\n> **\"(8)(A) For purposes of paragraphs (2), (6), and (7) of this subsection, and for no other purpose under this title, the current monthly income of a covered education debtor is the greater of the amount determined under section 101(10A) or the debtor's anticipated monthly income.**\n>\n> **\"(B) A covered education debtor is a debtor \u2014**\n>\n> **\"(i) whose debts include a qualified education loan, as defined in section 221(d)(1) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, that is not excepted from discharge by section 523(a)(8) of this title; and**\n>\n> **\"(ii) whose case is commenced within 12 months after the date on which the debtor last ceased to be enrolled on at least a half-time basis at an institution attendance at which gave rise to a qualified education loan described in clause (i).**\n>\n> **\"(C) The debtor's anticipated monthly income is the greatest of the following, divided by 12 \u2014**\n>\n> **\"(i) the annual compensation payable to the debtor under any employment in effect on the date of the order for relief;**\n>\n> **\"(ii) the annual compensation stated in any written offer of employment that the debtor has received and has not declined as of that date; and**\n>\n> **\"(iii) the annual compensation payable under any contract for the personal services of the debtor in effect on that date.**\n>\n> **\"(D) Where the debtor has no employment, offer, or contract described in subparagraph (C) on the date of the order for relief, the debtor's anticipated monthly income is zero.**\n>\n> **\"(E) This paragraph does not affect the application of paragraph (2)(D), and a debtor to whom paragraph (2)(D) applies is not a covered education debtor.\"**"},{"anchor":"sec-8-5","heading":"\u00a7 8-5. Construction of section 8-4.","part":"TITLE VIII \u2014 A PRIVATE LOAN ANSWERS TO A LENDER'S JUDGMENT","body":"**(a)** Section 8-4 changes no standard for the discharge of any debt, and no debt is excepted from discharge by reason of it. It governs only whether a case may be dismissed, or converted with the debtor's consent, under section 707(b) of title 11, United States Code.\n\n**(b) The debtor to whom nothing happens.** A debtor who holds no employment, has received no written offer of employment, and is party to no contract for the debtor's personal services on the date of the order for relief is **unaffected by section 8-4**, whenever the case is commenced.\n\n**(c)** Nothing in section 8-4 applies for purposes of section 1325(b) of title 11, United States Code, or any other provision of that title.\n\n**(d)** Section 8-4 does not limit section 707(b)(3) of that title, section 523(a)(2) of that title, or any other provision governing bad faith, abuse, or fraud."},{"anchor":"sec-8-6","heading":"\u00a7 8-6. Application.","part":"TITLE VIII \u2014 A PRIVATE LOAN ANSWERS TO A LENDER'S JUDGMENT","body":"**(a)** The amendments made by sections 8-2 and 8-4 apply to a case commenced under title 11, United States Code, on or after the date of enactment of this Act.\n\n**(b)** The amendment made by section 8-2 applies to a debt described in the subparagraph struck by that section whenever incurred.\n\n**(c)** The amendment made by section 8-4 applies whenever the qualified education loan was incurred and whenever the debtor ceased to be enrolled, subject to the 12-month period stated in the paragraph added by that section."},{"anchor":"sec-8-7","heading":"\u00a7 8-7. Construction.","part":"TITLE VIII \u2014 A PRIVATE LOAN ANSWERS TO A LENDER'S JUDGMENT","body":"**(a)** Nothing in this title affects the treatment under section 523(a)(8)(A) of title 11, United States Code, of a loan made, insured, or guaranteed by a governmental unit, of a loan made under a program funded in whole or in part by a governmental unit or a nonprofit institution, or of an obligation to repay funds received as an educational benefit, scholarship, or stipend.\n\n**(b)** Nothing in this title limits any other exception to discharge under section 523(a) of that title, including the exception for a debt obtained by false pretenses, a false representation, or actual fraud."},{"anchor":"schedule","heading":"SCHEDULE","part":null,"body":"| Ref | Provision | Value | *(Reserved.)* |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| S-1 | \u00a7 3-3, sec. 461(e)(6)(B), period for the Secretary of the Treasury to furnish figures after transmission of the request and completer list | [___ days] | *(Reserved.)* |"}],"asOf":"2026-11-12"}}}};
